  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2025-07-06T21:06:44.545Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Please consider adding support for Meta Quest’s new microgestures, introduced in Horizon OS v74+.\n\nThese subtle hand motions (such as thumb swipes and taps) massively improve interaction for hand-tracking users. They're already available through the Meta XR SDK (OVRMicrogestureEventSource) and OpenXR extension XR_META_hand_tracking_microgestures.\n\nWhy this matters:\n\n-Microgestures act like a virtual D-pad – making UI navigation, avatar emotes, or locomotion much more precise and intuitive for Quest users without controllers.\n\n-It opens the door for more FBT-like expressiveness in hand-tracking-only scenarios.\n\n-They are already supported in Unity via Meta’s Interaction SDK – meaning it’s almost plug-and-play to implement in your current Unity-based input stack.\n\n-The Meta SDK handles gesture detection, so there’s minimal logic required on VRChat’s side – just map the events to internal actions or expose them to Udon/avatars.\n\n-Makes it easier to navigate through the game for people with disabilities.\n\nTechnical summary (for devs):\n\n-Meta’s SDK ≥ v74+ includes OVRMicrogestureEventSource (Unity C#)\n\n-OpenXR extension: XR_META_hand_tracking_microgestures\n\n-Works out-of-the-box with current hand tracking on Meta Quest 2/3/Pro\n\n-Already tested in Unity demos with negligible overhead\n\nSuggested implementation:\n\n-Integrate microgesture events into VRChat’s input system\n\n-Optionally expose gesture detection to Udon graphs for world/av interaction\n\n-Even basic support (e.g. emote menu, UI nav, gesture triggers) would drastically improve usability for controller-free players\n\nSummary:\n\nThis is a low-effort, high-reward feature. You already support hand tracking — this is the next natural step, and Meta has done the heavy lifting on the SDK side.\n\nThanks for considering!",
